{
  "slug": "design-memory-allocator",
  "title": "Design Memory Allocator",
  "topic": "data-structures",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "design / first-fit allocation over a free-run list with hash map bookkeeping",
  "statement": "You manage `memory` units of address space, addressed `0` through `memory - 1` and all free at the start. A list of commands `ops` arrives, and each command is read by its sign and magnitude and produces exactly one integer result, collected in command order.\n\n- `ops[i] > 0` - an allocation request for a block of `ops[i]` consecutive free units. Occupy the lowest-addressed run that is long enough and return its starting address; if no run is long enough, allocate nothing and return `-1`. A successful block is labelled `i + 1`, the 1-based position of the command that created it.\n- `ops[i] < 0` - release the block labelled `-ops[i]`. Return how many units were returned to the free pool, or `0` if no live block carries that label (never allocated, or already released).\n- `ops[i] == 0` - no operation, result `0`.\n\nReleased units are immediately available to later allocation commands, and adjacent free regions are equivalent to one larger region. Return the array of results, one entry per command.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= memory <= 200`, `1 <= ops.length <= 200` and `-200 <= ops[i] <= 200`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "memory = 1, ops = [-200]",
      "output": "[0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "memory = 72, ops = [188]",
      "output": "[-1]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(memory: number, ops: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Two different pictures work: one integer per memory unit recording its owner, or a list of regions. The first is easy to get correct, the second is what makes release cheap.",
    "For the region picture you need two indexes at once: where a block starts, and the free list sorted by address so the lowest fit is the first one you meet.",
    "When a block is released next to a free run, the two must become one run. Otherwise a later request for their combined size is wrongly refused."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force models memory as one integer per unit, holding the label of the block that owns it or 0 when free, plus two maps from label to start and size. Allocation tries every start address in increasing order and verifies the whole run with an inner loop, which is O(memory) starts times O(size) units checked; release walks the block's units and clears them. The optimal stops tracking individual units and tracks regions: a sorted list of free half-open runs [start, end) and a map from label to a live block. Allocation walks the free runs in address order and returns the first whose length is at least the request, shrinking or deleting that run. Release looks the block up by label, deletes it, then inserts its span back into the free list and merges with an immediately preceding run ending at the span's start and an immediately following run starting at its end.",
    "why_optimal": "Each command touches only proportional to the number of live regions rather than every unit: an allocation scans the free list, which has at most memory runs, and a release is a map lookup plus an insertion that merges at most two neighbours, so the whole run is O(ops.length * memory). First-fit is inherently a scan of the candidate runs, since the lowest-addressed fit can only be found by looking from the bottom up, so O(memory) per allocation is the natural floor for this rule. The brute force's extra factor of `size` comes from re-verifying every unit of every candidate run, work the interval representation makes unnecessary.",
    "pitfalls": "Merging is the part that breaks: after splicing out the left neighbour, the index used to test the right neighbour shifts down by one, so checking the right neighbour with the pre-splice index skips a merge and leaves two touching runs that later fit a request they should not. Labels must be the command position, not a running counter of allocations, otherwise a release command cannot be matched to its block and `0` is returned for live memory. Freeing twice must return 0 the second time, which only works if the label is removed from the live map on release. An allocation request larger than `memory` must return -1 without touching state, and the allocation loop must stop at s + size <= memory to avoid reading past the end."
  },
